=== di" deletes inside the quotes
ref:    motion.txt *i`*
quote:  Like a", a' and a`, but exclude the quotes
given:  say "he|llo" now
keys:   di"
expect: say "|" now
regs:   "" = "hello"
undo:   say "|hello" now

=== da" deletes the quotes and the white space after them
ref:    motion.txt *a`*
quote:  Any trailing white space is included, unless there is none, then leading white space is included.
given:  say "he|llo" now
keys:   da"
expect: say |now
regs:   "" = "\"hello\" "

=== da" at the end of the line takes the white space before the string
ref:    motion.txt *a`*
quote:  Any trailing white space is included, unless there is none, then leading white space is included.
given:  say "h|i"
keys:   da"
expect: sa|y
regs:   "" = " \"hi\""

=== da" followed by punctuation takes no white space after
ref:    motion.txt *a`*
quote:  Any trailing white space is included, unless there is none, then leading white space is included.
given:  f( "a|b");
keys:   da"
expect: f(|);
regs:   "" = " \"ab\""

=== di" before the first quote on the line uses the first string
ref:    motion.txt *a`*
quote:  "\"a quoted string\". Selects the text from the previous quote until the next quote."
given:  |x = "abc"
keys:   di"
expect: x = "|"
regs:   "" = "abc"

=== di" with the cursor on the opening quote
ref:    motion.txt *a`*
quote:  When the cursor starts on a quote, Vim will figure out which quote pairs form a string by searching from the start of the line.
given:  x |"abc" y
keys:   di"
expect: x "|" y
regs:   "" = "abc"

=== di" with the cursor on the closing quote
ref:    motion.txt *a`*
quote:  When the cursor starts on a quote, Vim will figure out which quote pairs form a string by searching from the start of the line.
given:  x "abc|" y
keys:   di"
expect: x "|" y
regs:   "" = "abc"

=== di" on the opening quote of the second string
ref:    motion.txt *a`*
quote:  Vim will figure out which quote pairs form a string by searching from the start of the line.
given:  "a" |"b"
keys:   di"
expect: "a" "|"
regs:   "" = "b"

=== di" between two strings uses the white space between them
ref:    oracle nvim v0.11.7
given:  "a" | "b"
keys:   di"
expect: "a"|"b"
regs:   "" = "  "

=== di" skips an escaped quote
ref:    motion.txt *a`*
quote:  The 'quoteescape' option is used to skip escaped quotes.
given:  x "a\"|b" y
keys:   di"
expect: x "|" y
regs:   "" = "a\\\"b"

=== di" with no quotes on the line does nothing
ref:    motion.txt *a`*
quote:  Only works within one line.
given:  foo |bar
keys:   di"
expect: foo |bar
regs:   "" = (unset)

=== di" does not reach a quote on another line
ref:    motion.txt *a`*
quote:  Only works within one line.
given:
  "a
  |b"
keys:   di"
expect:
  "a
  |b"

=== d2i" deletes the quotes but no white space
ref:    motion.txt *i`*
quote:  Special case: With a count of 2 the quotes are included, but no extra white space as with a"/a'/a`.
given:  x "a|b" y
keys:   d2i"
expect: x | y
regs:   "" = "\"ab\""

=== di" on an empty string does nothing
ref:    motion.txt *i`*
quote:  Like a", a' and a`, but exclude the quotes
given:  x "|" y
keys:   di"
expect: x "|" y

=== ci" on an empty string inserts between the quotes
ref:    motion.txt *i`*
quote:  Like a", a' and a`, but exclude the quotes
given:  x "|" y
keys:   ci"ab<Esc>
expect: x "a|b" y
undo:   x "|" y

=== ci" replaces the string contents and repeats with dot
ref:    motion.txt *i`*
quote:  Like a", a' and a`, but exclude the quotes
given:  "a|b" "cd"
keys:   ci"x<Esc>
expect: "|x" "cd"
dot:    "|x" "cd"

=== yi" yanks the string contents and moves to their start
ref:    motion.txt *i`*
quote:  Like a", a' and a`, but exclude the quotes
given:  x "ab|c"
keys:   yi"
expect: x "|abc"
regs:   "0 = "abc"

=== di' deletes inside single quotes
ref:    motion.txt *i`*
quote:  Like a", a' and a`, but exclude the quotes
given:  x 'a|b' y
keys:   di'
expect: x '|' y
regs:   "" = "ab"

=== da' deletes the single-quoted string and trailing white space
ref:    motion.txt *a`*
quote:  Any trailing white space is included
given:  x 'a|b' y
keys:   da'
expect: x |y
regs:   "" = "'ab' "

=== di` deletes inside backticks
ref:    motion.txt *i`*
quote:  Like a", a' and a`, but exclude the quotes
given:  run `l|s -l` now
keys:   di`
expect: run `|` now
regs:   "" = "ls -l"

=== da` deletes the backtick string and trailing white space
ref:    motion.txt *a`*
quote:  Any trailing white space is included
given:  run `l|s` now
keys:   da`
expect: run |now
regs:   "" = "`ls` "

=== di" ignores other kinds of quotes
ref:    motion.txt *i`*
quote:  Like a", a' and a`, but exclude the quotes
given:  "it's |ok"
keys:   di"
expect: "|"
regs:   "" = "it's ok"
